change:
  id: CHG-032
  title: A generation ranks one candidate per distinct realised tree
  status: proposed
  intent: Decide candidate sameness by what landed, not by what the proposer claimed. Live run nw4 (2026-11-07) realised one identical file under three model-chosen ids and ranked it as a population of three with spread 0.00. Q-013's (id, patch) rule cannot see this. The realised Git tree of each candidate commit is the identity; a later candidate whose tree matches an earlier one is recorded as a duplicate of it and not ranked.
  audience_level: developer
  relates_to:
    - Q-013
    - ADR-0002
    - PAT-004

deltas:
  - op: MODIFIED
    capability: mutation-fitness-loop
    requirement: In a generation, two candidates are the same when their committed Git trees are identical. The first one evaluated ranks; each later one is recorded as a duplicate naming the candidate it repeats, is excluded from the ranking, the winner and the spread, and is shown in the journal and console. A repeated (id, patch) no longer aborts the generation, because it realises the same tree and is recorded as a duplicate.
    scenarios:
      - id: S1
        when: three candidates with different mutation ids realise the identical file
        then: the ranking holds one candidate, the other two are recorded as duplicates of it, and the generation's size is still three
      - id: S2
        when: two candidates realise different files
        then: both rank, whatever their mutation ids
      - id: S3
        when: a generation records duplicates
        then: the ledger has a row per duplicate naming the run, the duplicate candidate and the candidate it repeats, and the journal and console each show one line per duplicate
      - id: S4
        when: the same mutation id and patch repeat, as from a non-varying fixture proposer
        then: the generation completes with the repeat recorded as a duplicate, rather than aborting

acceptance:
  - scenario: S1
    test_id: cli/EvolveGenerationAcceptanceTest.candidatesThatRealiseTheSameFileRankOnce
    layer: acceptance
  - scenario: S2
    test_id: deterministic/GenerationEvaluationLoopTest.candidatesWithDifferentTreesBothRankWhateverTheirIds
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S3
    test_id: adapters/SqliteExperimentMetadataStoreIntegrationTest.recordsEachDuplicateAndTheCandidateItRepeats
    layer: integration
  - scenario: S4
    test_id: deterministic/GenerationEvaluationLoopTest.aRepeatedMutationIsRecordedAsADuplicateRatherThanAborting
    layer: unit

tasks:
  - id: T1
    slice: Acceptance test seen red first; real Git worktrees, OpenAI-compatible stub returning three ids over one file
    status: completed
  - id: T2
    slice: Deterministic port RealisedTreeIdentity (Candidate to tree id) and a Git adapter reading the commit's tree
    status: completed
  - id: T3
    slice: GenerationEvaluationLoop dedupes by tree; RankedGeneration gains duplicates; the (id, patch) abort is removed
    status: completed
  - id: T4
    slice: Ledger table for duplicates through schema_migrations; journal and console duplicate lines
    status: completed
  - id: T5
    slice: Q-013 resolved, ADR-0002 (both copies) and README note the rule
    status: pending

non_goals:
  - skipping checks for a duplicate before it is evaluated; checks are local and cheap, and the model cost is already spent
  - comparing trees across generations or runs
  - semantic sameness (two different files with the same behaviour)

risks:
  - The whole committed tree is compared, so a realiser that writes anything run-specific into the tree would hide every duplicate. Today the realiser writes only the mutated file.
  - Removing the (id, patch) abort means a non-varying proposer no longer fails loudly; it shows as a generation of one ranked candidate and N-1 duplicates. Accepted because the duplicate lines make it visible and a ranking of distinct trees has no tie artefact.
